```

which generates:

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "current": {
      "id": "jobId",
      "type": "string"
    },
    "previous": {
      "description": "previous job id",
      "type": "string"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "current",
    "previous"
  ]
}
```

Which isn't too bad but the ideal behaviour would I think be:

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "current": {
      "$ref": "#/$defs/jobId"
    },
    "previous": {
      "$ref": "#/$defs/jobId"
      "description": "previous job id",
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "current",
    "previous"
  ],
  "$defs": {
    "jobId": {
      "id": "jobId",
      "type": "string"
    }
  }
}
```

However, this might require a change to how `.meta()` works. Ideally every call would have to create a new instance rather than overwriting the previous version in the store.

WIth `zod-openapi` I made the decision to create new instances with every successive `.meta()/.openapi()` metadata but also merged previous data but discarded the `id` property after I received some requests/bug reports. This produced some pretty awesome reusable schema: https://github.com/samchungy/zod-openapi/releases/tag/v4.0.0

