## Summary

The MCP tool `find_subnet_for_task` ([`src/mcp-server.mjs`](src/mcp-server.mjs#L1146)) ranks the **entire** subnet index, truncates to a fixed pool of 50, and **only then** filters down to subnets that actually expose callable services. When 50+ non-callable subnets out-rank a callable one for the task term, the callable subnet is silently cut from the pool — so the tool returns **fewer than `limit` results, or zero**, and emits the misleading note *"No callable subnet matched this task."* even though a callable subnet genuinely matches.

This is the inverse of the order used by the sibling tool `find_subnets_by_capability` ([`src/mcp-server.mjs`](src/mcp-server.mjs#L629-L654)), which filters `callable_count > 0` **before** `.slice(0, limit)`. The asymmetry is the bug: a goal-shaped tool whose entire contract is *"return subnets that can do this task"* must apply the callability filter before the pool is cut, not after.

## Where

`rankSubnetsForTask` (keyword branch) — [`src/mcp-server.mjs:281-290`](src/mcp-server.mjs#L281-L290):

```js
const ranked = docs
  .filter((doc) => doc.type === "subnet")
  .map((doc) => ({
    netuid: doc.netuid,
    relevance: scoreDocument(doc, terms),
  }))
  .filter((entry) => entry.relevance > 0)
  .sort((a, b) => b.relevance - a.relevance || a.netuid - b.netuid)
  .slice(0, poolSize);          // <-- truncates to 50 BEFORE callability is known
return { mode: "keyword", ranked };
```

The handler then joins this already-truncated pool against the callable catalog — [`src/mcp-server.mjs:1149-1175`](src/mcp-server.mjs#L1149-L1175):

```js
const { mode, ranked } = await rankSubnetsForTask(ctx, task, 50);
const catalog = await loadArtifactData(ctx, "/metagraph/agent-catalog.json");
const byNetuid = new Map((catalog.subnets || []).map((entry) => [entry.netuid, entry]));
const results = [];
for (const { netuid, relevance } of ranked) {
  const entry = byNetuid.get(netuid);
  if (!entry) continue;          // <-- callability filter, AFTER the slice(0, 50)
  results.push({ /* ... */ });
  if (results.length >= limit) break;
}
```

`search.json` (the rank source) contains **all** enriched subnets; `agent-catalog.json` (the join target) contains **only** subnets exposing callable services. So the callability constraint is applied a full step too late — after the top-50 cut has already discarded callable candidates.

## Why it's wrong

`find_subnets_by_capability` demonstrates the correct order — it ranks over the catalog and filters callability **before** truncating ([`src/mcp-server.mjs:646-654`](src/mcp-server.mjs#L646-L654)):

```js
.filter((entry) => entry.score > 0 && entry.subnet.callable_count > 0)   // filter first
.sort(/* ... */)
.slice(0, limit);                                                         // then truncate
```

`find_subnet_for_task` does the opposite. Because the truncation (`poolSize = 50`) happens before callability is consulted, any callable subnet whose relevance ranks it at position …

### Component

src / feeds (`src/feeds.mjs`)

### Description

`clamp` truncates feed text with `String.prototype.slice`, which counts **UTF-16 code units**:

```js
function clamp(value, max = 500) {
  const s = stripControl(value).trim();
  return s.length > max ? `${s.slice(0, max - 1)}…` : s;
}
```

A non-BMP character (emoji, many CJK/math symbols — anything ≥ U+10000 is a surrogate pair of 2 code units) that straddles the `max - 1` boundary is sliced in half, leaving a **lone surrogate**. (Note `stripControl` already iterates by code point — `for (const ch of …)` — precisely to be code-point-correct; `clamp` then undoes that with a code-unit slice.)

#### Where

`src/feeds.mjs`, `clamp` (line ~53). `clamp` feeds both feed **titles** (`clamp(path, 80)` / `clamp(surface.surface_id, 80)`) and **summaries** (default `max = 500`) in `registryItems` / `incidentItems`.

#### Trigger / impact

A registry/incident feed entry whose name or artifact path contains a non-BMP character at the truncation boundary. Example: an artifact path of 78 ASCII chars + an emoji places the emoji's surrogate pair across the 80-char title clamp; `clamp(path, 80)` keeps only its high surrogate. Lone surrogates are **invalid in XML 1.0**, so the serialized RSS/Atom feed (`rssFeed` / `atomFeed`) is no longer well-formed and strict feed readers reject the whole document; on the UTF-8 wire the surrogate becomes U+FFFD (`�`), silently corrupting the text.

#### Reproduction

`registryItems({ artifacts: { modified: [{ path: "a".repeat(78) + "😀" + "z".repeat(20) }] } })` produces an item whose `title` ends in a lone high surrogate.

#### Proposed fix

Truncate by code points, consistent with `stripControl`:

```js
function clamp(value, max = 500) {
  const s = stripControl(value).trim();
  if (s.length <= max) return s;
  return `${[...s].slice(0, max - 1).join("")}…`;
}
```

Add a regression test asserting a feed title whose emoji straddles the 80-char clamp contains no lone surrogate.

