const message = 'Test timed out in 60000ms.\nIf this is a long-running test, pass a timeout value as the last argument or configure it globally with "testTimeout".';

const error = new Error(message);
error.stack = tmpl.stack.replace(error.message, tmpl.message); // as shipped
console.log(error.stack.split("\n")[0]);
// -> Error: STACK_TRACE_ERR…

### Describe the bug

`browser.connectTimeout` only takes effect on the root `test` config. Setting it inside a project is silently ignored: no warning, no error, and the default 60s is used instead.

The value is read here, in the browser session handshake:

```js
const timeout = setTimeout(() => {
  defer.reject(new Error(`Failed to connect to the browser session "${sessionId}" [${project.name}] within the timeout.`));
}, project.vitest.config.browser.connectTimeout ?? 6e4).unref();
```

`project.vitest.config` is the **root** resolved config, so a per-project `browser.connectTimeout` never reaches it even though every other `browser` option on that project (`enabled`, `provider`, `instances`, `headless`) is honoured, and the error message itself is interpolated with `project.name`.

That combination makes the per-project form look like the natural place to put it.

### Reproduction

Ignored, falls back to the 60s default:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    projects: [
      {
        test: {
          name: 'client',
          browser: {
            enabled: true,
            provider: playwright(),
            connectTimeout: 180_000,   // ignored
            instances: [{ browser: 'chromium', headless: true }]
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
});
```

Honoured:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    browser: { connectTimeout: 180_000 },   // honoured
    projects: [ /* … */ ]
  }
});
```

To observe it you need a run that actually fails the handshake. With the per-project setting, every failure landed at ~64s (the 60s default plus startup). Moving the identical value to the root config moved them to ~183s. Nothing else changed between the two runs.


Either of these would have saved the debugging time:

1. Resolve `connectTimeout` per project, like the other `browser.*` options, since the timeout and its error message are both per-session and the message already names the project; or
2. Keep it root-only and warn when a project sets it, the way an unknown or misplaced option normally would.



