## Summary

`IdentifyRecurringTransactionsJob` can perform repeated database lookups while identifying recurring transaction patterns for a family. Performance monitoring has shown N+1 query traces in this job around recurring transaction lookup and manual recurring variance updates.

## Details

Two paths currently scale with the number of detected or manual recurring patterns:

- each detected pattern can perform its own `family.recurring_transactions.find_or_initialize_by(...)` lookup;
- each active manual recurring transaction can call `find_matching_transaction_entries(...)`, which repeats a date/currency/name or merchant lookup per row.

For families with many recurring subscriptions or manually tracked recurring rows, the job does more database round-trips than necessary even though the work is already scoped to one family.

## Proposed direction

- Load existing recurring rows for the family once and match detected patterns in memory.
- Batch recent candidate entries for manual recurring variance recalculation, then filter by account, currency, merchant/name, and expected day in Ruby.
- Preserve existing behavior for recurring transfers, which still need pair-aware matching and should not be updated through the single-account variance path.
- Add regression coverage that asserts the old per-pattern and per-manual-row query shapes do not return.

## Acceptance criteria

- recurring transaction identification avoids the repeated recurring row lookup;
- manual recurring variance updates avoid one entry lookup per manual recurring row;
- family/account scoping is preserved;
- month-boundary recurring dates still match consistently;
- existing recurring transfer skip behavior remains intact;
- model/job tests cover the query-shape regressions.
